ha-ani-weather-card — derived from, not bundled
-----------------------------------------------

Every component named above is an npm dependency compiled into the bundle.
This one is not, and the distinction is the point of this entry: the Animated
Weather widget was written by reading ha-ani-weather-card
(https://github.com/alexsto1an/ha-ani-weather-card), Copyright (c) 2025
Animated Weather Card, distributed under the MIT License, and porting parts of
it. No file of that project is copied in or linked against. That card is Lit
and GSAP and emits a stylesheet; the Dashable widget draws React and Framer
Motion, and the rendering, the motion budget and the element caps are Robot One
Software's own. What was ported is substantial enough that the MIT notice below
travels with it:

  * the OKLCH sky palette tables — a colour set for each weather condition at
    each time of day — the dawn / day / dusk / night phase derivation built on
    them, the OKLCH-to-sRGB conversion, and the compositing and contrast maths.
    From the card's src/sky.ts, into src/lib/animatedWeather/sky.ts.

  * the weather-condition classification (from src/utils.ts, getWeatherClass)
    and the readable condition names (from src/weather-data.ts), both into
    src/lib/animatedWeather/motion.ts.

  * the discipline of computing the weather note's facts on the client — when
    rain starts and stops, the high and the low and when each lands, gusts, UV
    — so that a language model only phrases figures it has been handed and is
    never asked to invent a time or a temperature. From src/note-generator.ts,
    into src/lib/animatedWeather/facts.ts.

The src/lib/animatedWeather paths are paths in the Dashable viewer source
published at https://github.com/robot-one-software/dashable-viewer, and each of
those files repeats this attribution in its own header. This repository carries
the result compiled into
custom_components/dashable/frontend/dashable-panel.js.

